<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-uniform-and-lipschitz-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Uniform and Lipschitz continuity
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> -\infty \lt a \lt b \lt \infty </m> be real numbers, and consider the real-valued function <m> f \colon \clint{ a }{ b } \to \R </m> defined by the formula
      
      <md>
        f \of{ x } = x^{ 3 }
      </md>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that <m> f </m> is not uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that for any positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m>, 
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ f \of{ x + \frac{ \delta }{ 2 } } , f \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \of{ x + \frac{ \delta }{ 2 } }^{ 3 } - x^{ 3 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ \frac{ \delta }{ 2 } x^{ 2 } + \frac{ \delta^{ 2 } }{ 4 } x + \frac{ \delta^{ 3 } }{ 8 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        is the absolute value of a quadratic function of <m> x </m> and is therefore unbounded from above. This implies that for any positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m>, there is some real number <m> x \in \R </m> so that <m> d \of{ f \of{ x + \frac{ \delta }{ 2 } } , f \of{ x } } \geq 1 </m>, and so <m> f </m> is not uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Without appealing to <xref ref="proposition-lipschitz-continuity-implies-uniform-continuity"/>, verify that <m> f </m> is not Lipschitz continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that 
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 3 } - x_{ 2 }^{ 3 } } \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } \cdot \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 2 } + x_{ 1 } x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 }^{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 2 } + x_{ 1 } x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 }^{ 2 } } \cdot d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \R </m>. Since <m> x_{ 1 }^{ 2 } + x_{ 1 } x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 }^{ 2 } </m> is a quadratic polynomial in <m> x_{ 1 } </m> and <m> x_{ 2 } </m>, it is not bounded in absolute value, and so <m> f </m> cannot be Lipschitz continuous.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that the restriction of <m> f </m> to a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subsetneq \R </m> is uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number, and consider the positive real number
        
        <md>
          \delta = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ a }^{ 2 } , \abs{ b }^{ 2 } } } }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 3 } - x_{ 2 }^{ 3 } } \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } \cdot \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 2 } + x_{ 1 } x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 }^{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \cdot 3 \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ a }^{ 2 } , \abs{ b }^{ 2 } } } \lt 3 \delta \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ a }^{ 2 } , \abs{ b }^{ 2 } } } = \epsilon
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m> so that <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. Thus the restriction of <m> f </m> to <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> is uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that the restriction of <m> f </m> to a closed interval <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subsetneq \R </m> is Lipschitz continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 3 } - x_{ 2 }^{ 3 } } \amp = \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } \cdot \abs{ x_{ 1 }^{ 2 } + x_{ 1 } x_{ 2 } + x_{ 2 }^{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \cdot 3 \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ a }^{ 2 } , \abs{ b }^{ 2 } } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in \clint{ a }{ b } </m>. Thus the restriction of <m> f </m> to <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> is Lipschitz continuous, with Lipschitz constant
        
        <md>
          L_{ f } \leq 3 \max \of{ \set{ \abs{ a }^{ 2 } , \abs{ b }^{ 2 } } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
